ust the  fraction you had invested in each stock  times how much that stock value rows are  depreciated just add it up over all  stocks so this is the ratio of your  wealth for one particular day  and what you want to do is invest  sequentially over n days  uh which entails constructing this  portfolio selection strategy which is  basically a collection of function that  on day number t takes uh the price  relative vectors of days one through t  minus one and tells you  how uh you should what fraction of your  wealth should you invest in each stock  so on each day you get a probability  distribution over one through m  and if you sequentially invest over n  days the ratio of your wealth finally to  on day number one is just the product of  your ratios each day and then it turns  out to be this value  and what you're hoping for is that this  sn  is large  right so perhaps the simplest example of  a portfolio selection strategy is the  so-called constant rebalance portfolio  or crp which assigns equal fraction  uh sorry which assigns the same fraction  of your wealth to a particular stock  each day so this is completely time  invariant right so this is one of the  simplest examples of the portfolio  strategy you could have  right so now let me go on and exactly  formulate the problem so this was the  definition of crps and we can argue  separately that crps are a very  reasonable benchmark class to compare  yourself against so a reasonable goal  that we can set for ourself  is to have is to create a portfolio  strategy that  achieves wealth that's comparable to the  wealth of the best constant rebalance  portfolio in hindsight even for the  worst possible case of markets to market  vectors right so so this is the  portfolio regret for a strategy a and  what you want is that this regret should  be small  and we can write this in another form by  writing down what s n is that might be  more familiar this log of product just  becomes sum and what you end up getting  is this equivalent form which looks more  like an online learning problem against  the best fixed action in hindsight for  the worst possible case of inputs and  the loss function here is the special  kind of loss sometimes called the log  loss and so this is just online learning  with this loss  okay so that was the problem formulation  let me now tell you about tom cover's  solution to this problem called  universal portfolios and really the crux  of this idea is to understand is to use  the relationship between gambling and  data compression more precisely we use  uh the fact like the fact is that  sequential portfolio selection is  equivalent to universal compression  let's look at this equivalence a bit  more closely  so how do you go from a universal  compressor to a universal portfolio  selection method it's by something  called probability induced portfolios  and how you go from portfolio selection  method to a universal compressor is by  inputting these extremely kelly price  relatives which are basically it's a  price relative that's one at just one  component and zero everywhere else so if  you remember slide one this is the case  when you're betting on a sports  tournament and each day just one team is  going to win so portfolio selection with  these input vectors turns into universal  compression and cover shows us that in  some sense the most difficult market  that you can deal with the worst  sequence of price relatives you can deal  with is when everything is uh  in the form of a kelly vector so  gambling on a horse race is the most  difficult stock market to deal with  even more precisely the equivalence is  that portfolio selection against the  best crp  is exactly the same as a universal  compression of an emory sequence against  the best  fixed iid probability right  and  so covert strategy that  makes a probability induced portfolio  out of the universal compressor is able  to achieve half log n times m minus 1  regret which is optimal including the  constant this is unsurprisingly also the  optimal redundancy that you can achieve  when you're trying to compress an mre  sequence against the best id probability  right so this was towards universal  portfolios so now i'm going to  complicate this problem a little bit and  tell you about what we studied which is  the case when at time t in addition to  the yesterday's price relatives you have  some  extra side information zt that you can  incorporate so this could be anything  a canonical example that is usually  given is the truncated history of the  market but it could even be something  additional like in cover and or dental  original  example it was how oil prices might  affect the stock market if you're  betting on a cricket match it could be  the condition of the pitch on a  particular day  anything  so we also assume that there exists a  state function g that maps the site  information to one of s states right and  this state function is not known to the  investor so you don't know how the site  information is going to map to a state  and this is what makes this work  different from coburn or dental x  portfolio selection with site  information because they consider the  case when the state is directly visible  to everyone so there's no function g in  that case  okay so in this case we can also  strengthen the benchmark and uh  allow it both the flexibility to choose  us choose and know the state function g  and also to play a statewide crp so  earlier the uh  the benchmark was restricted to using  the same uh vector at each uh the same  crp at each time step but now the crp is  allowed to adapt to the state in this  manner  and we can have this this other this  regret formulation in this case which is  very similar except that over here  we have uh best statewide crp and here  is your portfolio incorporating the site  information  right  and perhaps a very simple example of a  strategy that um  a benchmark strategy that can adapt to  site information is when you just look  at yesterday's price relatives and  decide the state according to that so  for example you could say that if  yesterday's price relative for stock one  was 1.5 then today i'm going to invest  80 percent of my wealth in stock one  otherwise i'm going to just do uniformly  at random everything so this is one  example of how you could incorporate  state into this problem  so let me just state our main result in  words so  if the following conditions are met so  firstly if the site information process  is what i've written here as stationary  plus  because it's almost the same as  stationarity a little more stringent but  quite close  so if this first condition and the  second condition that the class of state  functions has finite nitrogen dimension  which if you haven't heard of it's just  a generalization of the vc dimension to  function classes that are supported on  more than two outputs because in our  case we might possibly have more than  two states so if these two conditions  are met then we're able to construct a  portfolio selection strategy that  achieves sublinear expected wealth ratio  um  and the exact non-asymptotics depend on  the nature and properties of this  process said and  right so that was one problem that i've  worked on so before winding up let me  just tell you  real quickly about other topics that  i've worked on previously so i worked on  various topics in information theory  including universal compression  sequential uh prediction portfolio  selection and so on  information measures which is about  understanding fundamental properties of  entropy and mutual information polar  codes and so on and i'm super excited to  be here and learn from everyone about  more topics in sequential decision  making  so very related to this presentation one  topic that i'm very interested in is  understanding online learning based  methods for sequential prediction and  exactly how they differ  from compression based methods in  sequential